---
import Footer from "../../components/shared/Footer.astro";
import Header from "../../components/shared/Header.astro";
import PageHeader from "../../components/shared/PageHeader.astro";
import ContactBanner from "../../components/shared/ContactBanner.astro";
import LeadBlock from "../../components/shared/LeadBlock.astro";
import Icon from "../../components/shared/Icon.astro";
import WtTrackerDiagram from "../../components/case-studies/WtTrackerDiagram.astro";
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { organization, siteUrl } from "../../data/organization";

const schema = [
  organization,
  {
    "@type": "SoftwareSourceCode",
    name: "wt-tracker-rust",
    description:
      "Multi-core WebTorrent tracker in Rust for WebRTC signaling in P2P networks, with zero-copy message handling and its own WebSocket implementation.",
    url: `${siteUrl}case-studies/wt-tracker`,
    codeRepository: "https://github.com/novage/wt-tracker-rust",
    programmingLanguage: "Rust",
    license: "https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0",
    author: { "@id": organization["@id"] },
  },
  {
    "@type": "SoftwareSourceCode",
    name: "wt-tracker",
    description:
      "WebTorrent tracker for WebRTC signaling in P2P networks, built on Node.js and µWebSockets.js.",
    codeRepository: "https://github.com/novage/wt-tracker",
    programmingLanguage: "TypeScript",
    license: "https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0",
    author: { "@id": organization["@id"] },
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="wt-tracker: Multi-Core WebTorrent Tracker in Rust | Novage"
  canonical="case-studies/wt-tracker"
  schema={schema}
  description="How we built wt-tracker in Rust with AI in two days: ~200k WebTorrent peers on a free 2-core Oracle Cloud server, ~100k within the free traffic."
>
  <Header />
  <div id="content">
    <article>
      <PageHeader
        eyebrow="Case study"
        title="wt-tracker"
        accent="Multi-Core WebTorrent Tracker in Rust"
      >
        <p class="lead">
          How we ported our WebTorrent tracker from Node.js to Rust with AI in
          two days, and built a server that can handle about{" "}
          <strong>200,000 simultaneous peers</strong> on a free 2-core Oracle
          Cloud instance (estimated) — and why that matters for the cost of P2P
          video.
        </p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a
            class="button primary"
            href="https://github.com/novage/wt-tracker-rust"
          >
            <Icon name="github" class="inline-icon" />
            View on GitHub
          </a>
          <a
            class="button secondary"
            href="https://github.com/novage/wt-tracker"
          >
            <Icon name="github" class="inline-icon" />
            Node.js version
          </a>
          <a
            class="button secondary"
            href="#contact"
            data-umami-event="wt-tracker-discuss"
          >
            Discuss your project
          </a>
        </div>
      </PageHeader>

      <div class="page container content">
        <ul class="key-facts">
          <li>
            <strong>~200,000</strong> simultaneous WebSocket Secure peers
            (estimated); ~100,000 within the free traffic, with a moderate
            tracker configuration that doesn't affect P2P
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>2 vCPU, 12 GB</strong> Ampere A1, Oracle Cloud Free Tier
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>2 days</strong> from the first commit to production, with
            AI-assisted development
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Apache-2.0</strong> open source
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>The challenge</h2>
        <p>
          Viewers in a <a href="/p2p-media-loader/overview">P2P Media Loader</a>{" "}
          network find each other through a WebTorrent tracker. Every viewer
          keeps a WebSocket connection open to the tracker, announces the
          streams it is watching, and exchanges WebRTC connection offers and
          answers (SDP) with other viewers of the same stream — a process called
          WebRTC signaling.
        </p>
        <p>
          Public trackers are fine to start with, but a production audience
          needs its own. The load is unusual: tens of thousands of long-lived
          TLS connections and a constant flow of small JSON messages, where
          every announce can fan out into offers to many other peers.
        </p>
        <p>
          Our goal was to serve 100,000 viewers from a free server, as one more
          step toward making video delivery affordable for anyone. Oracle
          Cloud's Always Free Tier offers a generous free server, but it has
          been halved: its Arm-based Ampere A1 instance now has 2 cores and 12
          GB of memory.
        </p>

        <h2>Where we started</h2>
        <p>
          Our first tracker,{" "}
          <a href="https://github.com/novage/wt-tracker">wt-tracker</a> for
          Node.js, keeps its logic in TypeScript and hands HTTP, WebSocket and
          TLS to{" "}
          <a href="https://github.com/uNetworking/uWebSockets.js">
            µWebSockets.js
          </a>
          , a native C++ networking stack. It is efficient per core, but Node.js
          runs it on one core, so on a 2-core server half of the CPU stays idle.
          Aquatic, a multi-threaded tracker in Rust, used 1.46 of the 2 cores at
          42,000 peers on the same server. Neither could reach 100,000.
        </p>
        <p>
          So we ported wt-tracker to Rust. The Node.js implementation remains
          available, and both use the same configuration file.
        </p>

        <h2>Ported with AI, verified against the original</h2>
        <p>
          AI coding agents wrote most of the code; our engineers set the goals,
          made the design decisions and reviewed every change. From the first
          commit to real viewers in production took <strong>two days</strong>.
          Speed did not come at the expense of correctness:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>A written specification</strong> is the source of truth for
            behavior, and it changes together with the code.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Differential testing:</strong> random message sequences go
            through both implementations, and the Rust tracker's replies must be
            byte-identical to the Node.js tracker's.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Twin benchmarks:</strong> every Rust benchmark has an
            identical JavaScript twin.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Conformance and fuzzing:</strong> all 517 cases of the
            Autobahn WebSocket testsuite pass, and the WebSocket framing,
            compression, HTTP upgrade and tracker protocol are fuzzed.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>CI</strong> runs all of it on every change, with longer
            fuzzing every night.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>How we made it fast</h2>

        <figure class="diagram-figure">
          <WtTrackerDiagram />
          <figcaption>
            Every peer keeps a WebSocket connection open. Each core runs a
            worker with its own WebSocket layer and its own swarms, and a
            connection moves to the worker that holds its stream.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h3>Multi-core without locks</h3>
        <p>
          Each CPU core runs a worker with its own share of the swarms, so
          workers never wait on each other.
        </p>

        <h3>Swarms follow the content</h3>
        <p>
          The video, audio and every quality of one stream live on one worker,
          and a viewer's connection moves to that worker at its first announce.
          Almost every message is then handled on a single core, with no traffic
          between threads.
        </p>

        <h3>Its own WebSocket implementation</h3>
        <p>
          Instead of µWebSockets, wt-tracker has its own WebSocket and TLS layer
          built for this workload. Like µWebSockets, it reads every connection
          through one shared buffer per worker, so a connection costs about 5–9
          KB of memory. Because we control it, it can also move a live
          connection, TLS session included, to another core — something
          µWebSockets doesn't offer, and what lets swarms follow the content. In
          our load tests on a single core, it needs up to a third less CPU per
          message than the Node.js implementation on µWebSockets.js and about
          3–5× less memory per connection.
        </p>

        <h3>Zero-copy messages</h3>
        <p>
          WebRTC offers and answers are forwarded as slices of the received
          message, never copied or re-encoded.
        </p>

        <h3>Less traffic per peer</h3>
        <p>
          On a free server, outbound traffic is the scarcest resource. Outgoing
          offers are compressed when the browser supports it, and reconnecting
          viewers resume their TLS session instead of downloading the
          certificate chain again. One peer costs about 38 bytes per second.
        </p>

        <h3>Proven ideas from the Node.js implementation</h3>
        <p>
          Swarm operations take constant time, whether a swarm has ten peers or
          ten thousand. Offers fan out from a random position in the swarm with
          no shuffling or temporary arrays. One sweep per announce interval
          removes silent peers instead of a timer per peer. Connection limits
          and allowed origins are checked during the HTTP upgrade, before a
          WebSocket is even created.
        </p>

        <h2>The results</h2>
        <p>
          On a 2-core Oracle Cloud Always Free Ampere A1 server with real P2P
          Media Loader viewers, at about 42,000 peers:
        </p>
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th></th>
              <th>Aquatic</th>
              <th>wt-tracker</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>CPU</td>
              <td>1.46 cores</td>
              <td>0.35 cores</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Memory</td>
              <td>1.9 GB</td>
              <td>590 MB</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>About 200,000 peers</strong> on the free server's 2 cores
            and 12 GB (estimated); about 100,000 within the free 10 TB of
            monthly outbound traffic.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>No complex horizontal scaling</strong> for many P2P video
            projects: one free server replaces a cluster.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Private by design:</strong> self-hosting keeps information
            about your audience and P2P network on your own infrastructure.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Production features:</strong> the WebTorrent "scrape"
            extension, Prometheus metrics and structured logs, allowed and
            denied origins, connection limits, a custom index page and graceful
            restarts.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          How we tuned the server and Oracle's network, including the setting
          that otherwise drops new connections at about 40,000 peers, is
          described in our article{" "}
          <a href="/blog/100k-webtorrent-peers-on-the-oracle-cloud-always-free-tier">
            100k WebTorrent Peers on the Oracle Cloud Always Free Tier
          </a>
          .
        </p>

        <h2>What it shows</h2>
        <p>
          A rewrite used to be a months-long bet. With AI-assisted development,
          held to a written specification, differential tests against the
          original and conformance suites, it took two days to move a production
          server to a new language — and the result does on one free server what
          used to need a paid cluster.
        </p>

        <h2>Get wt-tracker</h2>
        <p>
          wt-tracker is free and open source under the Apache-2.0 license. It
          works with <a href="/p2p-media-loader/overview">P2P Media Loader</a>{" "}
          and any WebTorrent-compatible client. Installation, configuration and
          deployment are covered in the{" "}
          <a href="https://github.com/novage/wt-tracker-rust#readme">
            README on GitHub
          </a>
          . The{" "}
          <a href="https://github.com/novage/wt-tracker">
            Node.js implementation
          </a>{" "}
          remains available for Node.js setups.
        </p>

        <LeadBlock title="Need a backend that does more with less?">
          wt-tracker is one example of the performance work we do for clients:
          profiling real traffic, redesigning hot paths and memory use, and
          letting one small server do the job of a cluster — for real-time
          systems, APIs and streaming backends. We also run and scale tracker
          infrastructure for P2P video in production.
        </LeadBlock>
      </div>
    </article>
    <ContactBanner />
  </div>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
